INT. LINCOLN HIGH SCHOOL - GIRLS' BATHROOM - DAY

SUPER: "DAY 11 OF THE SIEGE"

Cracked tile. A mirror with one long diagonal fracture. Dust sifts from the ceiling with every distant THUMP.

Outside, beyond the frosted window, smoke. Somewhere a siren winds down and stops.

NADIA, 17, backpack on her chest, crouches in the end stall with her knees pulled up. She counts under her breath.

ON PHONE (TEXT FROM MOM): where r u

ON PHONE (TEXT FROM MOM): please

No bars. The message sits there, sending.

CLOSE ON the phone's battery icon. 4%.

The door SLAMS open. Nadia flinches. KOFI, 17, flashlight clipped to his collar, pushes in backwards, dragging LUCAS, 16, who is limping, by the strap of his bag. Kofi kicks the door shut and wedges a mop handle under the knob.

KOFI
(whispering)
Girls' bathroom. Seriously?

LUCAS
It was the only one with a lock.

Silence.

A drip. A fan that doesn't turn.

NADIA
(from the stall)
It doesn't have a lock.

Kofi spins, flashlight up. Nadia opens the stall door an inch. She lowers her phone.

KOFI
Nadia?

NADIA
Put the light down.

He lowers it. Lucas slumps against the sinks and slides to the floor, wincing.

INSERT - LUCAS'S ANKLE. Swollen, black at the edges.

The wall behind them SHUDDERS. Plaster falls. A long, low rumble passes through the floor, and the three of them freeze at once, hands on whatever is nearest, until it passes.

Dust. Ringing. Nothing.

MS. BRANDT (O.S.)
(through the door, muffled)
Is anyone in there?

All three look at each other. Kofi holds a finger to his lips and mouths NO. Nadia nods.

MS. BRANDT (O.S.) (CONT'D)
It's Ms. Brandt. I can hear you breathing. Let me in.

LUCAS
(mouthing)
It's a trick.

NADIA
(whispering)
It's not a trick. It's Brandt.

KOFI
(whispering)
Brandt left on the first bus.

Silence. The handle turns. Stops against the mop.

On the floor, light spills from under the door in a slow, even sweep. Something glides past outside, and then another, and a third.

THE JUNIORS, a dozen kids in the corridor, press flat to the wall, quiet as shadows, led by a hand-sign nobody dares to say aloud. They pass the door. They are gone.

ON PHONE (TEXT FROM MOM): I love you. whatever happens

Nadia reads it. She doesn't type back. She presses the phone to her chest.

Kofi crawls to the door, listens, lets out a long breath. Lucas grabs his sleeve.

LUCAS
Don't open it.

KOFI
I'm not opening it.

NADIA
(quiet, cold)
Then why are you still touching the mop?

He looks down. His hand is on the handle. He pulls it back.

MS. BRANDT (O.S.)
(close now, right behind the door)
Last chance, kids. They are coming through the east wing.

The lights flicker. The mirror finishes cracking. A long, thin piece falls and shatters in the sink.

Then a loud, clear BANG from the corridor, and every one of them dives under the sinks, arms over each other, as the door rattles and the mop handle holds.

Smoke curls under the door.

Nobody moves.

Nobody speaks.

Nothing.

Nadia's phone buzzes. She does not look.

ON PHONE (TEXT FROM MOM): I'm coming
